```json
{
  "verdict": "inconsistent",
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "high",
      "quote": "TOTAL DUE: $540.00",
      "issue": "The line items sum to $515.00 ($240.00 + $180.00 + $95.00), so the stated total overstates the amount owed by $25.00."
    }
  ]
}
```

The three charges themselves are clear; only the total is wrong. Ask Hartley Plumbing for a corrected invoice at $515.00 before paying.

```json
{
  "verdict": "inconsistent",
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "high",
      "quote": "Sales tax (8%): $84.00",
      "issue": "8% of the $1,200.00 subtotal is $96.00, not $84.00. The stated $84.00 is 7% of the subtotal."
    },
    {
      "severity": "medium",
      "quote": "Total: $1,284.00",
      "issue": "The total is arithmetically consistent with the wrong tax figure ($1,200.00 + $84.00). At the stated 8% rate the total should be $1,296.00."
    }
  ]
}
```

One of the two numbers - the 8% rate or the $84.00 tax - does not belong. Confirm which rate actually applies before paying.

```json
{
  "verdict": "inconsistent",
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "medium",
      "quote": "TOTAL: $1,050",
      "issue": "The four categories sum to $1,150 ($300 + $450 + $250 + $150). The stated total understates the budget by $100."
    }
  ]
}
```

If you plan the month against $1,050, you will come up $100 short against your own category figures.

```json
{
  "verdict": "inconsistent",
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "high",
      "quote": "Sale price: $90.00",
      "issue": "The sale price equals the original price. A 30% discount on $90.00 would give a sale price of $63.00; no discount has actually been applied."
    }
  ]
}
```

Either the percentage or the price is wrong - as listed, the "sale" saves nothing.

```json
{
  "verdict": "inconsistent",
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "high",
      "quote": "Your fourteen chairs will ship on Friday.",
      "issue": "The order table specifies a quantity of 12 chairs, and the line total of $1,020 is correct for 12 x $85. The summary paragraph says fourteen chairs, contradicting the quantity you are being charged for."
    }
  ]
}
```

The billing is consistent with 12 chairs; the shipping summary is the odd one out. Confirm the quantity before the order ships.

```json
{
  "verdict": "inconsistent",
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "medium",
      "quote": "Qty 6 x $45.00 = $240.00",
      "issue": "6 x $45.00 is $270.00, not $240.00. Either the quantity, the unit price, or the extended price is wrong; $240.00 would correspond to a unit price of $40.00."
    }
  ]
}
```

The error is in your favor if the $240.00 stands, but the line as written does not multiply out - flag it so the invoice can be corrected cleanly.

```json
{
  "verdict": "inconsistent",
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "high",
      "quote": "$28 per guest x 60 guests = $1,540",
      "issue": "$28 x 60 is $1,680, not $1,540. The quote understates the multiplication by $140."
    }
  ]
}
```

Do not sign expecting $1,540 - on the quote's own numbers the charge should be $1,680, and the discrepancy will surface at final billing if it is not corrected now.

```json
{
  "verdict": "inconsistent",
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "medium",
      "quote": "Total due for the quarter (3 months): $4,300",
      "issue": "Three months at $1,450 is $4,350, not $4,300. The quarterly total is $50 less than the monthly rent implies."
    }
  ]
}
```

Confirm whether the monthly rent or the quarterly total is the typo before paying either figure.

```json
{
  "verdict": "inconsistent",
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "medium",
      "quote": "Year-to-date total: 420 units",
      "issue": "The three quarters sum to 400 units (120 + 150 + 130). The stated year-to-date total overstates sales by 20 units."
    }
  ]
}
```

Unless a fourth figure is missing from the memo, the year-to-date total should read 400 units.

```json
{
  "verdict": "inconsistent",
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "high",
      "quote": "February 31, 2027",
      "issue": "This date does not exist. February has 28 days in 2027 (29 in a leap year) and never has a 31st day, so the agreement's commencement date is impossible as written."
    }
  ]
}
```

The twelve-month term cannot be calculated from a start date that never occurs. Get the commencement date corrected - February 28 or March 1, 2027 - before signing.

```json
{
  "verdict": "inconsistent",
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "high",
      "quote": "Saturday, June 15, 2026",
      "issue": "June 15, 2026 falls on a Monday, not a Saturday. The weekday and the date in the invitation disagree."
    }
  ]
}
```

Decide which one is intended - Saturday, June 13, or Monday, June 15 - and correct the other before printing, or guests will arrive on two different days.

```json
{
  "verdict": "inconsistent",
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "high",
      "quote": "Phase 2 (build): completes April 15, 2026",
      "issue": "The build phase is scheduled to complete on April 15, 2026, five weeks before the design phase it depends on completes on May 20, 2026. The phases are out of order."
    }
  ]
}
```

Phase 3's June 30 date is consistent with a May design finish; the Phase 2 date is the one that cannot stand. The plan needs a corrected build completion date after May 20.

```json
{
  "verdict": "inconsistent",
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "high",
      "quote": "Date: November 31, 2026",
      "issue": "November has 30 days, so November 31, 2026 is not a real date. The invoice is dated on a day that does not exist."
    }
  ]
}
```

Because the payment terms run "30 days from invoice date," the impossible date also makes the due date uncomputable. Ask the sender to reissue with a valid date - November 30 or December 1, 2026.

```json
{
  "verdict": "inconsistent",
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "high",
      "quote": "Load-in: February 30, 2027",
      "issue": "February 30 does not exist in any year. February 2027 has 28 days, so the scheduled load-in day is impossible."
    },
    {
      "severity": "high",
      "quote": "Event date: Monday, February 14, 2027",
      "issue": "February 14, 2027 falls on a Sunday, not a Monday. The weekday stated in the contract disagrees with the date."
    }
  ]
}
```

Both date problems sit in the same short contract, which suggests the dates were typed from memory rather than a calendar. Confirm the real event day and load-in day with the venue and have the contract reissued.

```json
{
  "verdict": "inconsistent",
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "high",
      "quote": "Flexible terms - cancel anytime with no penalty.",
      "issue": "This contradicts Clause 3, under which the lease auto-renews for successive twelve-month terms and can only be stopped by notice given at least 90 days before the current term ends. A tenant who misses that window is committed for another year - the opposite of cancelling anytime."
    }
  ]
}
```

The summary box describes a month-to-month arrangement; the operative clause describes a self-renewing annual lease. Trust the clause, and ask the landlord to correct the summary before you sign.

```json
{
  "verdict": "inconsistent",
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "high",
      "quote": "No commitment - cancel anytime!",
      "issue": "The terms directly below impose a 12-month minimum term and charge the full remaining term on early cancellation. That is a year-long commitment with a substantial exit fee - the opposite of what the headline promises."
    }
  ]
}
```

The terms, not the headline, are what you would be held to. Treat this as a 12-month contract with an expensive exit, whatever the headline says.

```json
{
  "verdict": "inconsistent",
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "high",
      "quote": "Pets welcome!",
      "issue": "The summary is contradicted by Clause 14 of the lease itself, which prohibits animals of any kind on the premises. If you sign the lease as written, the clause - not the summary - is what binds you."
    }
  ]
}
```

Do not rely on the "pets welcome" line. Ask the landlord to amend Clause 14 in the signed lease before you commit, or your dog puts you in breach from day one.

```json
{
  "verdict": "inconsistent",
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "high",
      "quote": "No deductible - we cover 100% of costs from the first dollar.",
      "issue": "The policy schedule in the same document imposes a $500 deductible on all claims. A policy cannot both have no deductible and apply a $500 deductible to every claim."
    }
  ]
}
```

Budget as if the $500 deductible is real - the schedule is the operative part of the policy - and ask the insurer to correct the summary, which overpromises.

```json
{
  "verdict": "inconsistent",
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "high",
      "quote": "Our grand hall accommodates up to 200 guests for seated events.",
      "issue": "Clause 19 of the same contract caps occupancy of the hall at 120 persons including staff and vendors. The marketing figure in the introduction and the fire-safety limit in the contract differ by 80 people."
    }
  ]
}
```

No - not for 180 guests. The binding number is the 120-person fire-safety cap in Clause 19, and it includes your staff and vendors, so the hall legally holds fewer than 120 guests. Either the venue corrects the contract or you need a different hall; do not rely on the 200 figure in the introduction.

```json
{
  "verdict": "inconsistent",
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "medium",
      "quote": "We bring all supplies and equipment needed for each visit.",
      "issue": "The client checklist in the same agreement requires the client to provide a vacuum cleaner and all cleaning products. The two statements assign the same responsibility to opposite parties."
    }
  ]
}
```

Ask the company which version is current before the first visit. If you are expected to supply the products, the service is not quite the all-inclusive offer the agreement's opening line describes.

```json
{
  "verdict": "inconsistent",
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "high",
      "quote": "Spacious 1,200 sq ft 2-bedroom!",
      "issue": "The floor plan details in the same listing give the total living area as 950 sq ft. The headline overstates the size by 250 sq ft - more than a quarter more space than the detailed figures support."
    }
  ]
}
```

Price per square foot changes a lot between the two figures. Ask the agent which measurement is correct and how it was taken before relying on either.

```json
{
  "verdict": "inconsistent",
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "high",
      "quote": "Only 42,000 miles",
      "issue": "The service history in the same listing records the odometer at 58,300 miles in March 2026. A car's mileage cannot be lower now than a reading already recorded in its past, so the headline figure contradicts the car's own documented history."
    }
  ]
}
```

This is the kind of discrepancy that can indicate odometer rollback or a listing error. Do not proceed without an explanation and an independent mileage record.

```json
{
  "verdict": "inconsistent",
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "medium",
      "quote": "No experience required - we train the right person!",
      "issue": "The requirements section of the same posting mandates a minimum of 5 years of experience in a similar role. A posting cannot simultaneously require no experience and require five years of it."
    }
  ]
}
```

One of the two statements is a leftover from another posting. If you are an entry-level applicant, ask the employer which is real before spending time on the application.

```json
{
  "verdict": "inconsistent",
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "high",
      "quote": "Available March 1 - move in right away!",
      "issue": "The lease details in the same listing state that a current tenant occupies the unit through April 30 and the earliest move-in is May 1. The headline promises availability two months before the unit can actually be occupied."
    }
  ]
}
```

Do not give notice on your current place based on the March 1 headline. The binding constraint is the sitting tenant's April 30 end date - confirm the May 1 move-in with the landlord directly.

```json
{
  "verdict": "inconsistent",
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "medium",
      "quote": "Sleeps 6 comfortably!",
      "issue": "The beds listed in the same description sleep four people: two twin beds (one person each) plus one double bed (two people). There is no third bed, sofa bed, or additional sleeping arrangement mentioned anywhere in the listing."
    }
  ]
}
```

As described, the cabin sleeps 4, not 6. Message the host before booking - either a sleeping arrangement is missing from the description or two of your group are on the floor.

```json
{
  "verdict": "inconsistent",
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "high",
      "quote": "Flight 1: Arrives Denver 2:05 p.m. / Flight 2: Departs Denver 2:30 p.m.",
      "issue": "The connection is 25 minutes, but the booking itself states a minimum connection time of 45 minutes at this airport. The itinerary violates the minimum stated in the same document by 20 minutes."
    }
  ]
}
```

Do not pay for this as-is. A 25-minute connection that breaks the airline's own stated minimum usually means the second flight is not protected if the first runs late - ask for a later departure out of Denver.

```json
{
  "verdict": "inconsistent",
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "high",
      "quote": "Departs: 9:15 a.m. / Arrives: 8:50 a.m. (same day)",
      "issue": "The arrival time is 25 minutes before the departure time on the same day. A train cannot arrive before it leaves."
    },
    {
      "severity": "medium",
      "quote": "Journey time: 2 hours 35 minutes",
      "issue": "The stated journey time matches neither printed time: a 9:15 a.m. departure plus 2 hours 35 minutes gives an arrival of 11:50 a.m., not 8:50 a.m."
    }
  ]
}
```

The 11:50 a.m. arrival implied by the journey time is probably the real one and "8:50" the typo - but confirm with the rail operator, because the ticket as printed is impossible.

```json
{
  "verdict": "inconsistent",
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "high",
      "quote": "Flight: Lands 11:40 p.m.",
      "issue": "The flight lands 1 hour 40 minutes after the hotel's reception closes at 10:00 p.m., and the same confirmation states that late check-in is not possible and unclaimed rooms are released. As booked, you cannot check in on arrival night."
    }
  ]
}
```

This booking cannot be completed as written. Contact the hotel before travel to arrange a late arrival in writing, or move to a hotel with 24-hour reception - do not rely on landing early.

```json
{
  "verdict": "inconsistent",
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "high",
      "quote": "Your workshop ends at 5:00 p.m. Your airport shuttle departs the venue at 4:30 p.m.",
      "issue": "The shuttle leaves 30 minutes before the workshop it is meant to collect you from has ended."
    },
    {
      "severity": "high",
      "quote": "Your flight departs at 6:00 p.m. - please arrive at the airport 90 minutes before departure.",
      "issue": "Ninety minutes before a 6:00 p.m. flight is 4:30 p.m. at the airport - the exact moment the shuttle is only leaving the venue. Even with an instant journey, you cannot meet the email's own arrival instruction."
    }
  ]
}
```

As written, you must either leave the workshop early or miss the flight. Ask the organizer for a later shuttle, an earlier workshop finish, or a later flight - the email's own numbers leave no version of the day that works.

```json
{
  "verdict": "inconsistent",
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "high",
      "quote": "Revenue grew 8% this year",
      "issue": "The report's own table shows revenue falling from $1.25M in 2025 to $1.15M in 2026. That is a decline of $0.10M on a $1.25M base - a decrease of exactly 8%, not growth of 8%. The summary has the right magnitude and the wrong direction."
    }
  ]
}
```

The summary appears to have dropped a minus sign. Correct it before this goes to anyone - a summary claiming growth its own table refutes is the first thing a careful reader checks.

```json
{
  "verdict": "inconsistent",
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "medium",
      "quote": "A majority of users (48%)",
      "issue": "48% is not a majority. A majority means more than half - over 50%. 48% is a plurality at most (the largest share, if the rest is split), and it also means 52% of users did not choose the new design."
    }
  ]
}
```

Rewrite as "a plurality of users (48%)" or "the largest group of users (48%)" - or wait for a figure that actually clears 50% before claiming a majority.

```json
{
  "verdict": "inconsistent",
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "medium",
      "quote": "The median customer age is 34.",
      "issue": "The demographics table in the same report gives the median age as 43. The summary and the table disagree by nine years - the digits look transposed (34 vs 43), so one of them is a typo."
    }
  ]
}
```

The table's figures are internally coherent (a median of 43 with a mean of 41 is plausible), which makes the summary's 34 the likely error. Confirm against the source data and correct whichever is wrong.

```json
{
  "verdict": "inconsistent",
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "high",
      "quote": "95% of tickets were resolved within SLA.",
      "issue": "The report's own appendix gives 1,710 of 1,900 tickets resolved within SLA. 1,710 / 1,900 is exactly 90%, not 95%. The headline claim overstates performance by 5 percentage points."
    }
  ]
}
```

Either the counts in the appendix or the percentage in the claim is wrong. If the counts are right, the claim should read 90%.
